-- issues.rank (added by 0015) is generated by packages/envoy/internal/dispatch/rank, which
-- compares base-62 digits as raw bytes: 0-9 < A-Z < a-z. Every query that reads rank order
-- (lastProjectRank, nextProjectRank, previousProjectRank, the two "> "/"< " neighbor
-- comparisons in issue_rank.go, the issue list orderings in issue_queries.go and
-- architecture_tree.go) relies on that byte order. The column carried no explicit collation,
-- so comparisons and "order by" used the database's default collation, en_US.utf8 on both the
-- Aurora dispatch database (created with no LC_COLLATE) and CI's postgres:16 service. That
-- collation sorts case-insensitively with lowercase before uppercase on ties ("a" < "A" < "Z"),
-- which disagrees with rank's byte order as soon as a rank contains a lowercase digit.
--
-- Every rank ever written before this migration stays in [0-9A-Z] (index 0 to 35), by
-- induction: the pre-0015 backfill is zero-padded digits (index at most 9), and every
-- subsequent write built only on ranks already in that range — a two-sided midpoint averages
-- two digit indices already at most 35, so the average is too; `before` either recurses on a
-- smaller suffix of an existing rank or halves an existing digit index (at most 35, so the
-- half is too), and its base case is "U" (index 30); and the old append rule always appended a
-- literal "U" (index 30). None of those four ever reaches a lowercase index (36 or above), so
-- this migration reorders nothing already stored. `rank.after` (added alongside this
-- migration) is the first code that can write a lowercase digit, which is what makes the
-- mismatch reachable.
alter table issues alter column rank type text collate "C";
